{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MbzJhsi8gpBHk21gdgpThC","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Incident Report: ecommerce-api|Error\n2\t\n3\t| Field | Value |\n4\t|-------|-------|\n5\t| **Fingerprint** | `ecommerce-api|Error` |\n6\t| **Detected** | 2026-05-17 |\n7\t| **Status** | Resolved |\n8\t\n9\t## Summary\n10\t\n11\tA TOCTOU (time-of-check/time-of-use) race condition in `inventory.js:reserveInventory()` allowed three concurrent checkout requests for product 7 (Ceramic Plant Pot, stock=5) to simultaneously read the same stale stock value, all pass the availability check, and all decrement — driving in-memory stock to -3. Every subsequent checkout for that product immediately failed with HTTP 409 \"Insufficient stock … available -3\", producing a sustained ~19% error rate until the fix was deployed.\n12\t\n13\t## Timeline\n14\t\n15\t| Time (UTC) | Event |\n16\t|------------|-------|\n17\t| 2026-05-17 20:53:49 | First `ERROR` log recorded (prior pod run) |\n18\t| 2026-05-17 21:21:21.633 | `InventoryRaceCondition` logged — stock driven to -1 by concurrent decrement |\n19\t| 2026-05-17 21:21:21.638 | Second race resolves — stock reaches -3 (5ms later) |\n20\t| 2026-05-17 21:21:21 | All checkout requests for product 7 begin failing (stock permanently -3 in memory) |\n21\t| 2026-05-17 21:22:12 | Alert watcher fires at 19% error rate |\n22\t| 2026-05-17 21:22:12 | Phase 1 investigation started |\n23\t| 2026-05-17 21:22:12 | Root cause identified (race condition self-documented in source at line 30) |\n24\t| 2026-05-17 ~21:30:00 | Phase 2 fix proposed (synchronous re-check before decrement) |\n25\t\n26\t## Root cause\n27\t\n28\t`reserveInventory` in `otel-demo/ecommerce/backend/src/services/inventory.js` had a non-atomic check-then-act sequence separated by a 50–150ms simulated processing delay (`await`). Under concurrent load:\n29\t\n30\t1. All three requests called `getStock()` (5ms async delay) and read `stock = 5`.\n31\t2. All three passed the `currentStock < requested` check.\n32\t3. All three entered the 50–150ms `await` window simultaneously.\n33\t4. Decrements executed sequentially: `5 → 3 → 1 → -1 → -3`.\n34\t\n35\tThe post-write guard at line 68 detected -1 and -3 and emitted `InventoryRaceCondition` errors (2 HTTP 500 responses), but the in-memory store was already permanently at -3. All subsequent requests hit the pre-decrement check with `currentStock = -3` and returned HTTP 409 indefinitely.\n36\t\n37\t## Contributing factors\n38\t\n39\t- Limited stock (5 units) for product 7 made the race easy to trigger — any three concurrent orders of 2 units exhausted it.\n40\t- Three `load-generator` pods started concurrently with the API pod, providing sustained parallel checkout traffic.\n41\t- The in-memory inventory store is never reset between requests, so a single race event permanently corrupted state for the pod's lifetime.\n42\t\n43\t## Detection\n44\t\n45\tAutomated alert watcher detected fingerprint `ecommerce-api|Error` when the 1-minute error rate crossed the 15% threshold, reaching 19% at 2026-05-17T21:22:12Z. Root cause was confirmed via ClickHouse trace and log queries — `InventoryRaceCondition` log entries at 21:21:21 with explicit `stock_before=5` / `stock.actual=-3` attributes, corroborated by all 104 error-rate traces showing the `reserveInventory` span as the error origin.\n46\t\n47\t## Fix\n48\t\n49\tAdded a synchronous re-read of `inventory[item.id]` immediately before the decrement in `reserveInventory` (`inventory.js`). Because there is no `await` between the re-read and the write, Node.js's single-threaded event loop guarantees these two operations are atomic — no other request can interleave. If the re-read reveals insufficient stock (another request decremented during the processing delay), an \"Insufficient stock\" error is thrown before any write occurs, preventing negative stock.\n50\t\n51\tSee PR [#28](https://github.com/har-ki/claude-code-sre-handbook/pull/28) for the diff.\n52\t\n53\t## Action items\n54\t\n55\t- [ ] Replace the simulated processing delay with real async work or remove it — the delay exists solely to widen the race window and has no production value.\n56\t- [ ] Persist inventory to a backing store (e.g., Redis with atomic `DECRBY` / Lua script) so a pod restart is not required to recover from corrupted in-memory state.\n57\t- [ ] Add a `/admin/reset-stock` endpoint (or rely on `resetStock()`) with appropriate auth so on-call can recover without a pod restart.\n58\t- [ ] Lower the alert threshold from 25% to 15% (current trigger was already at 15%; verify the watcher config matches).\n59\t\n60\t## Open questions\n61\t\n62\t- Should `resetStock()` be called automatically when `InventoryRaceCondition` is detected, rather than waiting for a pod restart?\n63\t- Are other products with low initial stock (e.g., product 7 with stock=5) at disproportionate risk, and should they have higher reorder thresholds?\n64\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"52df716d-de27-46b1-89e3-0675eb8be203","uuid":"70833dba-22e0-45cb-b805-8b676d1166d8","timestamp":"2026-05-19T18:45:49.054Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/ecommerce/docs/incidents/2026-05-17-6e14cf73.md","content":"# Incident Report: ecommerce-api|Error\n\n| Field | Value |\n|-------|-------|\n| **Fingerprint** | `ecommerce-api|Error` |\n| **Detected** | 2026-05-17 |\n| **Status** | Resolved |\n\n## Summary\n\nA TOCTOU (time-of-check/time-of-use) race condition in `inventory.js:reserveInventory()` allowed three concurrent checkout requests for product 7 (Ceramic Plant Pot, stock=5) to simultaneously read the same stale stock value, all pass the availability check, and all decrement — driving in-memory stock to -3. Every subsequent checkout for that product immediately failed with HTTP 409 \"Insufficient stock … available -3\", producing a sustained ~19% error rate until the fix was deployed.\n\n## Timeline\n\n| Time (UTC) | Event |\n|------------|-------|\n| 2026-05-17 20:53:49 | First `ERROR` log recorded (prior pod run) |\n| 2026-05-17 21:21:21.633 | `InventoryRaceCondition` logged — stock driven to -1 by concurrent decrement |\n| 2026-05-17 21:21:21.638 | Second race resolves — stock reaches -3 (5ms later) |\n| 2026-05-17 21:21:21 | All checkout requests for product 7 begin failing (stock permanently -3 in memory) |\n| 2026-05-17 21:22:12 | Alert watcher fires at 19% error rate |\n| 2026-05-17 21:22:12 | Phase 1 investigation started |\n| 2026-05-17 21:22:12 | Root cause identified (race condition self-documented in source at line 30) |\n| 2026-05-17 ~21:30:00 | Phase 2 fix proposed (synchronous re-check before decrement) |\n\n## Root cause\n\n`reserveInventory` in `otel-demo/ecommerce/backend/src/services/inventory.js` had a non-atomic check-then-act sequence separated by a 50–150ms simulated processing delay (`await`). Under concurrent load:\n\n1. All three requests called `getStock()` (5ms async delay) and read `stock = 5`.\n2. All three passed the `currentStock < requested` check.\n3. All three entered the 50–150ms `await` window simultaneously.\n4. Decrements executed sequentially: `5 → 3 → 1 → -1 → -3`.\n\nThe post-write guard at line 68 detected -1 and -3 and emitted `InventoryRaceCondition` errors (2 HTTP 500 responses), but the in-memory store was already permanently at -3. All subsequent requests hit the pre-decrement check with `currentStock = -3` and returned HTTP 409 indefinitely.\n\n## Contributing factors\n\n- Limited stock (5 units) for product 7 made the race easy to trigger — any three concurrent orders of 2 units exhausted it.\n- Three `load-generator` pods started concurrently with the API pod, providing sustained parallel checkout traffic.\n- The in-memory inventory store is never reset between requests, so a single race event permanently corrupted state for the pod's lifetime.\n\n## Detection\n\nAutomated alert watcher detected fingerprint `ecommerce-api|Error` when the 1-minute error rate crossed the 15% threshold, reaching 19% at 2026-05-17T21:22:12Z. Root cause was confirmed via ClickHouse trace and log queries — `InventoryRaceCondition` log entries at 21:21:21 with explicit `stock_before=5` / `stock.actual=-3` attributes, corroborated by all 104 error-rate traces showing the `reserveInventory` span as the error origin.\n\n## Fix\n\nAdded a synchronous re-read of `inventory[item.id]` immediately before the decrement in `reserveInventory` (`inventory.js`). Because there is no `await` between the re-read and the write, Node.js's single-threaded event loop guarantees these two operations are atomic — no other request can interleave. If the re-read reveals insufficient stock (another request decremented during the processing delay), an \"Insufficient stock\" error is thrown before any write occurs, preventing negative stock.\n\nSee PR [#28](https://github.com/har-ki/claude-code-sre-handbook/pull/28) for the diff.\n\n## Action items\n\n- [ ] Replace the simulated processing delay with real async work or remove it — the delay exists solely to widen the race window and has no production value.\n- [ ] Persist inventory to a backing store (e.g., Redis with atomic `DECRBY` / Lua script) so a pod restart is not required to recover from corrupted in-memory state.\n- [ ] Add a `/admin/reset-stock` endpoint (or rely on `resetStock()`) with appropriate auth so on-call can recover without a pod restart.\n- [ ] Lower the alert threshold from 25% to 15% (current trigger was already at 15%; verify the watcher config matches).\n\n## Open questions\n\n- Should `resetStock()` be called automatically when `InventoryRaceCondition` is detected, rather than waiting for a pod restart?\n- Are other products with low initial stock (e.g., product 7 with stock=5) at disproportionate risk, and should they have higher reorder thresholds?\n","numLines":64,"startLine":1,"totalLines":64}}}
